Found while measuring karr #113 (sops 3.13.3 at /tmp/sops). NOT fixed by it.

karr #113 made rotate work on a sops-written YAML document holding a bare
.inf in an unencrypted slot: sops -e -> File::SOPS->rotate -> sops -d is exit
0 for all three spellings sops writes, wire byte-identical. edit is NOT, and
it fails for a different reason and in a different place.

MEASURED, editor that changes an unrelated key, before and after karr #113:

  .inf   edit CROAK: v_unencrypted: cannot write this leaf to a SOPS YAML
         document: its spelling is a YAML 1.2 constant ...
  -.inf  same
  .nan   same

That is ADR 0013s foreign-resolution guard, not the non-finite guard -- the
message is identical with karr #113s narrowing applied and with it bypassed.

MECHANISM: edit decrypts to a PLAINTEXT document, hands it to the editor, and
reparses what comes back. ADR 0026s repair walk deliberately does not run on a
plaintext document (it gates on the sops: section), so the leaf comes back as
the string .inf, and ADR 0013 correctly refuses to write a bare .inf that Go
would resolve to a float. Both guards are right about what they see; the value
lost its float-ness in the middle, on OUR OWN plaintext document.

Note edit with a no-op editor returns 0 without re-encrypting, so the failure
only shows when something actually changed.

The fix is a decision about ADR 0026s gate (its "Rejected alternatives", last
entry), and it is not the same one that ADR made: there the argument against
running the walk on plaintext was that a caller-supplied plaintext gets a
better error from ADR 0013. Here the plaintext is one this library WROTE from
a document it had already resolved, which is a different case and may deserve
a different answer -- possibly a marker on the way out rather than a re-guess
on the way in.

