WHY THE WIDE ANSWER. The ticket frames this as needing a way to tell edit's
intermediate plaintext from a foreign one. Measured, that distinction is not
needed and not wanted: ADR 0026's gate rested on "a worse message for no gain,
since neither path can write the document", and ADR 0031 (karr #113) made the
document writable three commits ago. With the gate on, this library's own
decrypt_file wrote `v_unencrypted: .inf` and its own encrypt_file refused to
read that file back -- a broken round trip with no editor in it at all. The
narrow flag was built in a scratch lib/ and measured first; it leaves edit and
encrypt_file answering oppositely to identical bytes, in BOTH directions.

WHAT A USER LOST. Not just an error: the edit itself. _edit_text scopes the
plaintext to a File::Temp dir that goes out of scope when it RETURNS, before
the re-encryption runs -- measured, the editor's file and its directory are
gone by the time the croak reaches the caller.

REFERENCE. `sops edit` on the same three documents with the same
non-interactive editor: 3 of 3 exit 0, wire unchanged, sops -d exit 0.

